<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-length-of-a-line-in-three-dimensions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Length of a Line in Three Dimensions</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.4 Three Dimensional Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Calculate the length of a line and projection of line on a plane in a 3-D object
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Lengths of edges, face diagonals and space diagonals in solids are found by repeated use of Pythagoras' theorem, e.g. the space diagonal of a cuboid = √(l² + w² + h²).
         Suggested activity: Learners measure the length, width and height of a carton (or the classroom), calculate the space diagonal, then check it with a string stretched corner to corner.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Cuboid with sliders for length, width and height; the face diagonal and space diagonal are drawn and their lengths calculated, with the two right-angled triangles shaded. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core 2.4 (b): lengths of lines and of their projections in solids
         Activity: as planning notes, the classroom option (sec-magnitude-of-a-vector-in-three-dimensions already uses the carton and string; a shoebox check in part (f))
         JSXGraph: fig-cuboid-diagonals (assets/jsxgraph/cuboid-diagonals.js), as planning notes; the triangles ABC and ACG are shaded by 2D polygons over view.project3DTo2D points
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/09-three-dimensional-geometry/03-length-of-a-line-in-three-dimensions/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> every length in a solid sits in a right-angled triangle; Pythagoras twice; the space diagonal <m>\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}</m>; a line, its projection and the perpendicular make a right-angled triangle.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find face and space diagonals, sloping edges and slant heights of pyramids, lengths of projections and a missing edge.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> rooms, water tanks, pencil boxes and cables.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find the longest straight line in the classroom by two uses of Pythagoras and check the method on a shoebox.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Groups measure the room with a tape measure; groups without one use the measurements in part (a). One group checks the method with string on a shoebox in part (f). Do not give the formula before part (e).</p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-corner-to-corner">
        <title>Corner to Corner Across the Classroom</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Label the classroom as a cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m>, with <m>A</m> a corner of the floor, <m>ABCD</m> the floor and <m>G</m> the ceiling corner furthest from <m>A</m>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-two-right-triangles">
                <caption>The floor diagonal <m>AC</m> and the diagonal <m>AG</m> of a classroom, and the two right-angled triangles that give them.</caption>
                <image width="90%">
                    <shortdescription>A classroom drawn as a cuboid ABCDEFGH, 10 m by 7 m by 3.5 m, with the floor diagonal AC dotted and the diagonal AG and the vertical edge CG drawn thick. Beside it, the triangle ABC is drawn flat with AB = 10, BC = 7, a right angle at B and hypotenuse root 149, and the triangle ACG is drawn flat with AC = root 149, CG = 3.5 and a right angle at C.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\tdplotsetmaincoords{70}{120}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{scope}[tdplot_main_coords, scale=0.32]
\coordinate (A) at (7,0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (7,10,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0,10,0);
\coordinate (D) at (0,0,0);
\coordinate (E) at (7,0,3.5);
\coordinate (F) at (7,10,3.5);
\coordinate (G) at (0,10,3.5);
\coordinate (H) at (0,0,3.5);
\draw[dashed] (D) -- (A);
\draw[dashed] (D) -- (C);
\draw[dashed] (D) -- (H);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (G);
\draw (G) -- (H);
\draw (H) -- (E);
\draw (E) -- (A);
\draw (E) -- (F);
\draw (F) -- (G);
\draw (B) -- (F);
\node[anchor=north east] at (A) {$A$};
\node[anchor=north] at (B) {$B$};
\node[anchor=north west] at (C) {$C$};
\node[anchor=south east] at (D) {$D$};
\node[anchor=east] at (E) {$E$};
\node[anchor=south east] at (F) {$F$};
\node[anchor=south west] at (G) {$G$};
\node[anchor=south] at (H) {$H$};
\draw[very thick, densely dotted] (A) -- (C); \draw[very thick] (A) -- (G); \draw[very thick] (C) -- (G);
\draw (0.459,9.345,0.000) -- (0.459,9.345,0.800) -- (0.000,10.000,0.800);
\draw (7.000,9.200,0.000) -- (6.200,9.200,0.000) -- (6.200,10.000,0.000);
\node[anchor=north] at (7,5,0) {$10$}; \node[anchor=north west] at (3.5,10,0) {$7$}; \node[anchor=west] at (0,10,1.75) {$3.5$};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(5.2,-1.6)}, scale=0.3]
\draw[thick] (0,0) node[anchor=north east]{$A$} -- (10,0) node[anchor=north west]{$B$} -- (10,7) node[anchor=south west]{$C$} -- cycle;
\draw (9.2,0) -- (9.2,0.8) -- (10,0.8);
\node[anchor=north] at (5,0) {$10$}; \node[anchor=west] at (10,3.5) {$7$}; \node[anchor=south east] at (5,3.5) {$\sqrt{149}$};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(9.2,-1.6)}, scale=0.3]
\draw[thick] (0,0) node[anchor=north east]{$A$} -- (12.207,0) node[anchor=north west]{$C$} -- (12.207,3.5) node[anchor=south west]{$G$} -- cycle;
\draw (11.407,0) -- (11.407,0.8) -- (12.207,0.8);
\node[anchor=north] at (6.1,0) {$\sqrt{149}$}; \node[anchor=west] at (12.207,1.75) {$3.5$};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Measure the length, width and height of the classroom. (If you have no tape measure, use <m>AB = 10\,\text{m}</m>, <m>BC = 7\,\text{m}</m> and <m>AE = 3.5\,\text{m}</m>.)</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The values in the solutions below use <m>10\,\text{m}</m>, <m>7\,\text{m}</m> and <m>3.5\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find the length of the floor diagonal <m>AC</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>AC^2 = 10^2 + 7^2 = 149</m>, so <m>AC = \sqrt{149} \approx 12.21\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Triangle <m>ACG</m> has a right angle at <m>C</m>, because <m>CG</m> is vertical. Find the length of <m>AG</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>AG^2 = AC^2 + CG^2 = 149 + 3.5^2 = 161.25</m>, so <m>AG = \sqrt{161.25} \approx 12.70\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What is the projection of <m>AG</m> on the floor? On the front wall <m>ABFE</m>? Find the length of the second projection.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>On the floor, <m>AC</m>. On the front wall, <m>G</m> projects to <m>F</m>, so the projection is <m>AF</m>: <m>AF^2 = 10^2 + 3.5^2 = 112.25</m>, <m>AF = \sqrt{112.25} \approx 10.59\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Show that <m>AG^2 = AB^2 + BC^2 + CG^2</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>AG^2 = AC^2 + CG^2</m> and <m>AC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2</m>, so <m>AG^2 = AB^2 + BC^2 + CG^2</m>. Check: <m>100 + 49 + 12.25 = 161.25</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Check the method on a shoebox: measure its three edges, calculate the diagonal from one bottom corner to the opposite top corner, then stretch a string between those corners inside the box and measure it.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Answers depend on the box. The string should agree with <m>\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}</m> to within a few millimetres; report both values honestly.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise lengths in solids as Pythagoras in right-angled triangles, used twice where needed.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> parts (b) and (c) used Pythagoras twice, first across the floor and then up; part (e) joined the two steps into one formula.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find the right-angled triangle, draw it flat with its right angle marked, then use Pythagoras.</p></li>
                <li><p>Keep squares such as <m>149</m> between steps instead of rounded lengths such as <m>12.21</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Real-world link:</alert> builders check that a room is square by measuring its diagonals, and a long pole is carried through a doorway along a diagonal.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-lengths-in-three-dimensions">
        <title>Lengths in Three Dimensions</title>
        <p>
            Find the right-angled triangle, then use Pythagoras; in a solid you often need it twice. In a cuboid with length <m>l</m>, width <m>w</m> and height <m>h</m>:
            <ul>
                <li><p>a <term>face diagonal</term> joins opposite corners of one face, for example <m>AC = \sqrt{l^2 + w^2}</m>;</p></li>
                <li><p>a <term>space diagonal</term> joins opposite corners of the cuboid, through its inside:
                    <md>AG = \sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}</md></p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            A line, its projection on a plane and the perpendicular from its end make a right-angled triangle, so
            <md>(\text{line})^2 = (\text{projection})^2 + (\text{rise})^2</md>
            In a right pyramid <m>VABCD</m>, the <term>sloping edge</term> <m>VA</m> has projection <m>OA</m> on the base, half a diagonal of the base. The <term>slant height</term> <m>VM</m>, to the midpoint <m>M</m> of <m>BC</m>, has projection <m>OM</m>, half of <m>AB</m>. As in Grade 10, the slant height is the height of a sloping face, which is not the same as the height <m>VO</m> of the pyramid.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <note xml:id="note-lengths-and-vectors">
        <title>Lengths and Vectors</title>
        <p>In <xref ref="sec-magnitude-of-a-vector-in-three-dimensions"/> the same calculation gives the length of a vector from its components.</p>
    </note>

    <figure xml:id="fig-pyramid-lengths">
        <caption>The sloping edge <m>VA</m> and the slant height <m>VM</m> of a right pyramid, with the right-angled triangles <m>VOA</m> and <m>VOM</m> drawn flat.</caption>
        <image width="80%">
            <shortdescription>A right pyramid VABCD on a square base of side 10 cm with height VO = 12 cm, VO dotted. OA and OM are dashed in the base, with M the midpoint of BC; VA and VM are thick. Beside it, triangle VOA is drawn flat with OA = root 50, VO = 12, VA = root 194 and a right angle at O, and triangle VOM with OM = 5, VO = 12, VM = 13 and a right angle at O.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\tdplotsetmaincoords{70}{120}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{scope}[tdplot_main_coords, scale=0.27]
\coordinate (A) at (10,0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (10,10,0);
