    <exercises xml:id="exercises-length-of-a-line">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-diagonals-of-a-cuboid">
            <statement><p>A cuboid measures <m>12\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>9\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>8\,\text{cm}</m>. Find the length of a diagonal of its <m>12\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>9\,\text{cm}</m> base, and of its space diagonal.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>15\,\text{cm}</m>; <m>17\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Base diagonal <m>\sqrt{144 + 81} = \sqrt{225} = 15\,\text{cm}</m>. Space diagonal <m>\sqrt{225 + 64} = \sqrt{289} = 17\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-diagonals-of-a-cube">
            <statement><p>A cube has edges of <m>5\,\text{cm}</m>. Find the length of a face diagonal and of a space diagonal.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\sqrt{50} \approx 7.07\,\text{cm}</m>; <m>\sqrt{75} \approx 8.66\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-edge-of-a-rectangular-pyramid">
            <statement><p>A right pyramid <m>VABCD</m> has a rectangular base <m>14\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>10\,\text{cm}</m> and height <m>9\,\text{cm}</m>. Find the length of the projection of <m>VA</m> on the base, and the length of <m>VA</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\sqrt{74} \approx 8.60\,\text{cm}</m>; <m>\sqrt{155} \approx 12.45\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>AC^2 = 14^2 + 10^2 = 296</m>, so <m>OA = \frac{1}{2}\sqrt{296} = \sqrt{74} \approx 8.60\,\text{cm}</m>. Then <m>VA^2 = 74 + 9^2 = 155</m>, so <m>VA = \sqrt{155} \approx 12.45\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-pencil-in-a-box">
            <statement><p>A geometry-set box is <m>18\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>5\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>4\,\text{cm}</m>. Will a pencil <m>19\,\text{cm}</m> long fit inside the closed box, lying straight?</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Yes, just: the space diagonal is <m>\sqrt{365} \approx 19.10\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>The longest straight line in the box is the space diagonal, <m>\sqrt{18^2 + 5^2 + 4^2} = \sqrt{365} \approx 19.10\,\text{cm}</m>. This is more than <m>19\,\text{cm}</m>, so the pencil fits along the diagonal, with very little to spare.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-height-of-a-store">
            <statement><p>A store room is <m>6\,\text{m}</m> long and <m>4\,\text{m}</m> wide. A pole <m>9\,\text{m}</m> long just fits from a floor corner to the opposite ceiling corner. Find the height of the room.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\sqrt{29} \approx 5.39\,\text{m}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>9^2 = 6^2 + 4^2 + h^2</m>, so <m>h^2 = 81 - 36 - 16 = 29</m> and <m>h = \sqrt{29} \approx 5.39\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-floodlight-cable">
            <statement><p>A floodlight stands on top of a <m>15\,\text{m}</m> pole at the centre of a square playground of side <m>40\,\text{m}</m> in Eldoret. A cable runs straight from the top of the pole to one corner of the playground. Find the length of the projection of the cable on the ground, and the length of the cable.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\sqrt{800} \approx 28.28\,\text{m}</m>; <m>\sqrt{1025} \approx 32.02\,\text{m}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>The projection runs from the centre of the square to a corner: half a diagonal. The diagonal squared is <m>40^2 + 40^2 = 3200</m>, so the projection is <m>\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{3200} = \sqrt{800} \approx 28.28\,\text{m}</m>. The cable is <m>\sqrt{800 + 15^2} = \sqrt{1025} \approx 32.02\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-spider-in-a-box">
            <title>Challenge: A Spider on a Box</title>
            <statement><p>A closed box is <m>30\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>20\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>10\,\text{cm}</m>.
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the length of its space diagonal.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A spider at one corner crawls over the outside of the box to the opposite corner. Find the length of the shortest crawl.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p></statement>
            <hint><p>Unfold two faces flat, so that the crawl becomes a straight line. There are three ways to choose the two faces.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>\sqrt{1400} \approx 37.42\,\text{cm}</m> (b) <m>\sqrt{1800} \approx 42.43\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) <m>\sqrt{30^2 + 20^2 + 10^2} = \sqrt{1400} \approx 37.42\,\text{cm}</m>.</p>
                <p>(b) Unfolded, the two faces make a rectangle, and the crawl is its diagonal. The three unfoldings give rectangles <m>30</m> by <m>20 + 10</m>, <m>20</m> by <m>30 + 10</m> and <m>10</m> by <m>30 + 20</m>, with diagonals <m>\sqrt{1800} \approx 42.43</m>, <m>\sqrt{2000} \approx 44.72</m> and <m>\sqrt{2600} \approx 50.99\,\text{cm}</m>. The shortest crawl is <m>42.43\,\text{cm}</m>, longer than the space diagonal, as it must be.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-length-of-a-line">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a table with columns <q>triangle</q>, <q>right angle at</q>, <q>sides known</q> and <q>side wanted</q>. Work through <xref ref="example-diagonals-of-a-water-tank"/> and <xref ref="example-edges-of-a-pyramid"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-diagonals-of-a-cuboid"/> and <xref ref="ex-diagonals-of-a-cube"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity plus all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-diagonals-of-a-cuboid"/> to <xref ref="ex-floodlight-cable"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-spider-in-a-box"/>. Extension: find whole-number edges for a box whose space diagonal is exactly <m>22\,\text{cm}</m>. (Answer: <m>4</m>, <m>12</m> and <m>18\,\text{cm}</m>, or <m>12</m>, <m>12</m> and <m>14\,\text{cm}</m>; for example <m>16 + 144 + 324 = 484 = 22^2</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

